      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mooresburg was founded in the early 1800s by Hugh Moore, who moved from North Carolina with his brothers to settle the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://easttennesseevisitorsguide.com/city-guide-mooresburg/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Major Hugh Moore, whose family established the community around 1800.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresburg%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "When the Tennessee Valley Authority dammed the Holston River in 1941, many Mooresburg residents were relocated to make way for Cherokee Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresburg%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mooresburg has never been incorporated; Hawkins County supplies most municipal services, and residents also rely on nearby Rogersville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://easttennesseevisitorsguide.com/city-guide-mooresburg/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During summer months, visitors flock to Quarry Cove, a lakefront glamping resort on Lake Cherokee that offers fully outfitted tents and a campervan site.",
